            maker listed on 19 October 2026 and that Garage Gym Reviews or BarBend had described in
            enough detail to cross-check. Weight tolerance, durometer, plate widths and warranties went
            on the cards only where the maker and a review agreed.
          </p>
          <p>
            That process removed two brands. Synergee no longer lists bumper plates on its own store —
            its catalogue showed bars, storage and collars but no plates when we checked — so we could
            not confirm a current Synergee bumper. Yes4All sells bumper plates mainly through Amazon;
            we could not open its listings, its own site did not return the products, and we found no
            independent review giving its tolerance or durometer, so there was nothing to check a card
            against. We also left out Rogue's Echo bumpers, Garage Gym Reviews' pick for home gyms,
            only because Rogue sells exclusively through its own store; they are a good alternative if
            you are buying a Rogue bar anyway.
          </p>
          <p>
            Some figures came off the cards. Fringe Sport lists a three-year warranty on its black
            bumpers' 25 lb and heavier plates and one year on the 10s and 15s, while Garage Gym Reviews
            describes a lifetime warranty, so the card gives Fringe's terms in the text and leaves the
            warranty out of the specification line. Garage Gym Reviews quotes a 92 Shore A durometer for
            REP's competition plates from an older listing; REP's current page gives none, so neither
            does the card.
          </p>
          <p>
            Every brand was checked against the CPSC recall database by brand name in recall titles,
            manufacturer fields and recall descriptions, and the category was searched for bumper
            plates and weight plates. We found no recalls of bumper plates or Olympic weight plates by
            any maker. The only REP recall we found is a 2021 recall of strap safety brackets for its
            racks, and we found none for Titan Fitness, Fringe Sport, CAP Barbell, Synergee or Yes4All.
            The weight-plate recalls in the database involve adjustable dumbbells whose plates can
            detach, which our <a href="/best-adjustable-dumbbells/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">adjustable dumbbell guide</a> covers in detail, and a 2021 recall of Impex Olympic dumbbell handles whose collars could
            slip.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bounce" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Bounce and Durometer</h2>
<p>
            Every bumper bounces a little. The question is how much, because a bar that rebounds hard
            off the floor can hop sideways, hit the rack or come back up towards the lifter's shins.
            In a garage it also makes more noise and passes more energy into the floor.
          </p>
          <p>
            Durometer is the usual measure. It is a hardness reading on the Shore A scale, from 0 to
            100: the higher the number, the harder the rubber and, broadly, the lower the bounce.
            Garage Gym Reviews treats readings in the 60s as moderately bouncy and 85 or above as a
            low, "dead" bounce. Fringe Sport publishes 90 Shore A for its 10 and 15 lb plates and 85
            for the 25 lb and heavier plates, on both its black and MilSpec bumpers, and Garage Gym
            Reviews repeats the same figures.
          </p>
          <p>
            Many makers publish no durometer at all. REP does not list one for its black bumpers,
            which BarBend notes as a drawback while describing the plates as low bounce in use; Titan
            gives none for its Economy plates, and Garage Gym Reviews calls their bounce "low-ish".
            Treat "low bounce" on a listing without a number as a claim, not a measurement.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two things affect bounce as much as the rubber. The floor matters: plates bounce more off
            a hard platform or concrete than off thick rubber mats. And the load matters: a heavy bar
            bounces less than a light one, which is why most people notice bounce most with a
            barbell carrying only a pair of 10s or 15s. Our <a href="/best-lifting-platforms/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lifting platform guide</a> explains how a stiff platform under rubber controls both bounce and noise.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="thickness" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Thickness and Bar Space: How Many Plates Fit?</h2>
<p>
            Bumpers are far thicker than iron plates of the same weight, because the rubber has to
            absorb the drop. That limits how much you can load. A typical 20 kg bar has about 16
            inches of loadable sleeve — REP lists 16.1 inches on the Colorado and Rogue 16.4 inches on
            the Ohio — and you need some of that for a collar.
          </p>
          <p>
            The arithmetic, using Fringe Sport's published widths: a 45 lb Fringe black bumper is 2.9
            inches thick, so five of them take 14.5 inches, leaving about 1.6 inches for a collar on a
            16.1-inch sleeve. That is 225 lb a side, or about 494 lb with a 20 kg bar — which is why
            Garage Gym Reviews says you cannot load more than about 500 lb of these plates on most bars.
            Fringe's 55 lb plate is about 3.2 inches, so five would fill the sleeve with no room for a
            collar; four 55s and a 25 is the practical maximum, about 245 lb a side. On a 15 kg bar
            with 12.4-inch sleeves, four 45 lb bumpers is the limit.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Plate</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">10 lb width</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">45 lb width</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Source</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Fringe Sport Black and MilSpec</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1.05 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2.9 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Fringe Sport; Garage Gym Reviews matches</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Titan Economy</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1 in. (25 mm)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2.91 in. (74 mm)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Titan; Garage Gym Reviews matches the 25 mm 10s</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">REP Black</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2.83 in. (BarBend's measurement)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">REP for the 10s; REP lists no 45 lb width</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            For most home lifters 450 to 500 lb of bumpers is more than enough. If you deadlift more
            than that, the usual answer is not thinner bumpers but iron plates on the inside for heavy
            pulls you lower under control, or competition plates, which are thinner for their weight.
            Our <a href="/best-weight-plates/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">weight plate guide</a> covers the iron side. Sleeve length also limits trap bars, many of which have shorter
            sleeves than a barbell; our <a href="/best-trap-bars/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">trap bar guide</a> gives the figures.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tolerance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Weight Tolerance: How Close to 45 lb Is a 45?</h2>
<p>
            Weight tolerance is how far a plate may be from its stated weight. Training bumpers are
            usually sold at ±1% to ±3%; competition plates at ±10 grams.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Tolerance</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">On one 45 lb plate</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Worst case across a pair</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Plates here</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">±10 g</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">±0.02 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0.04 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">REP Competition</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">±1%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">±0.45 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0.9 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">REP Black, Fringe Black, Fringe MilSpec</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">±3%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">±1.35 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2.7 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Titan Economy</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The worst-case column is arithmetic: one plate at the top of its tolerance and the other at
            the bottom. In practice most plates land closer than their stated tolerance, and a pound or
            two of difference between the two ends of a bar is not something most people can feel. It
            matters if you compete and need your training numbers to match a meet, if you like to track
            small jumps in weight, or if you are buying single plates over several years and want them
            to match. For everyone else, ±1% is comfortably enough, and ±3% is acceptable at the right
            price.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="smell-care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Smell, Grease and the 10 lb Plate Problem</h2>
<p>
            New rubber plates often smell, and some arrive greasy. Fringe Sport's own FAQ says the
            smell comes from the manufacturing process and is not permanent, suggests airing the plates
            in the sun for about five days, and says the grease is part of manufacturing and can be
            wiped off. BarBend mentions an initial odour on both REP's black plates and Fringe's MilSpec
            plates. Virgin
            rubber tends to smell less than recycled or crumb rubber, and if you are setting up in a
            living space, a basement without ventilation or a room next to a bedroom, it is worth
            choosing virgin rubber and unboxing the plates in a garage first.
          </p>
          <p>
            The 10 lb plates deserve their own check, because they are the most likely bumpers to bend.
            A thin 10 lb bumper dropped on its own on a bar can fold, or "taco". Makers deal with it in
            different ways. REP makes its 10s a full inch thick for durability. Fringe makes its black
            10s slightly smaller in diameter, 445 mm, so the heavier plates take the impact when they
            are loaded together, and says its 10s survive drops on their own. Titan's own Amazon
            listing for its Economy 10s warns that they are not designed to be used on their own on a
            bar. If you are learning the lifts with light weights, either buy 10s that are designed to
            be dropped alone or use a technique bar or training plates sized for it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Do not mix bumper types with different diameters on the same bar. REP says its bumper
            warranty is void if the plates are used with its smaller Hi-Temp plates, because the larger
            plate takes the whole impact; the same logic applies to mixing bumpers and iron plates.
            Store plates vertically on a rack or tree, not in a pile, and keep them out of direct
            sunlight once the smell has gone. Our <a href="/best-home-gym-accessories/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home gym accessories guide</a> covers plate storage and collars.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="competition-vs-training" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Competition vs Training Bumpers</h2>
<p>
            Competition bumpers are built to the International Weightlifting Federation's standard:
            ±10 g tolerance, colour-coded by weight (red, blue, yellow and green for 25, 20, 15 and 10
            kg, or their pound equivalents), a steel disc insert and a thinner profile for the weight.
            Training bumpers have a looser tolerance, are usually black, and are thicker and cheaper.
          </p>
          <p>
            REP's pound competition plates show the price difference clearly. On 19 October 2026 a pair
            of 45s cost $299.99, or about $3.33 a pound, against $199.99, about $2.22 a pound, for a
            pair of REP's black training 45s. For a home gym where nobody competes in weightlifting,
            the extra accuracy rarely earns that premium. It does if you compete, if you coach lifters
            who do, or if you want a set that will hold its accuracy and appearance for many years.
          </p>
          <p>
            Some "competition-style" colour plates are simply coloured training plates; check the
            tolerance before you pay for the look. Kilogram plates are the standard for weightlifting
            competition and pounds for most US gyms; if your bar is 20 kg and your plates are pounds,
            that is normal, but keep your logbook in one unit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="price-per-pound" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Price per Pound and Sets vs Pairs</h2>
<p>
            Per-pound prices are the fairest way to compare plates sold in different bundles. The
            figures below are our arithmetic from the prices on each maker's store on 19 October 2026,
            before any shipping charges or discount codes.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Plates</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Bundle priced</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Price</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Per lb</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">CAP Economy</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">160 lb set</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$167.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">about $1.05</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Fringe Sport MilSpec</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">pair of 45s</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$169.00</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">about $1.88</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Titan Economy</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">230 lb set (on sale)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$464.97</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">about $2.02</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">REP Black</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">pair of 45s</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$199.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">about $2.22</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Fringe Sport Black</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">160 lb set</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$399.00</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">about $2.49</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">REP Competition (LB)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">pair of 45s</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$299.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">about $3.33</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Two patterns stand out. Heavy plates are cheaper per pound than light ones — a pair of
            Fringe MilSpec 10s costs $55, or $2.75 a pound, against $1.88 for the 45s — so a set heavy
            in 45s looks cheaper per pound than one with many small plates. And sets are not always
            cheaper than the same plates bought as pairs: Fringe's MilSpec pairs of 10, 25, 35 and 45 lb
            add up to $462 for 230 lb, almost exactly the sale price of Titan's 230 lb set. Price the
            plates you actually need.
          </p>
          <p>
            For a first set, a 160 lb set (a pair each of 10s, 25s and 45s) covers most beginners for
            a while, and 230 to 260 lb with a pair of 35s or 15s gives finer steps. Add change plates
            for small jumps; bumpers usually start at 10 lb.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying Bumper Plates</h2>
          <p>Each of these is easy to avoid before you order and annoying to discover afterwards.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Forgetting how thick bumpers are</h3>
              <p>Bumpers take far more sleeve space than iron plates of the same weight. If you deadlift heavy, check the plate widths against your bar's loadable sleeve length before ordering, or plan to use iron plates for heavy lifts you lower under control.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Dropping thin 10s on their own</h3>
              <p>A pair of thin 10 lb bumpers dropped alone on a bar can fold. Check whether the maker designs its 10s to be dropped alone; if not, load them with heavier plates or use a technique bar and training plates for light work.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Dropping onto the wrong floor</h3>
              <p>Bumpers protect the bar and plates, not the floor. Dropped onto tile, hardwood or thin foam, a loaded bar can still crack or dent the surface beneath. Use a lifting platform or thick rubber flooring, and get a structural check before lifting heavy upstairs.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Mixing plates of different diameters</h3>
              <p>Loading bumpers with iron plates, or with thinner high-temperature bumpers, means the largest plate takes the whole impact, which damages it and can void the warranty. Keep each bar to one type of plate when you plan to drop it.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">For most home gyms, buy REP's black bumper plates</strong>: ±1%
            tolerance, durable inch-thick 10s and an Amazon listing, for about $2.22 a pound in 45s.
            Fringe Sport's black plates are the set to choose for published low-bounce figures, and its
            MilSpec plates, from Fringe's own store, are the best value, with 55s; its Savage pairs are
            the 55s to buy on Amazon. Titan's Economy plates suit buyers who want singles or 100s, REP's
            competition plates (kilograms on Amazon) suit lifters who compete, and CAP's Economy sets
            are the cheapest way in.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whatever you buy, check the plate widths against your bar, treat thin 10s with care, and
            give the plates something to land on — <a href="/best-lifting-platforms/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">our lifting platform guide</a> covers the surfaces that protect both the floor and the plates.
          </p>
        </div>

